{"content":"Perfect! Now I have all the evidence. Let me create a comprehensive answer.\n\nh05.\n\n**Mechanism:** Emoji reactions are removed by **explicit Go code** during user deletion.\n\n**Evidence locations:**\n\n1. **User deletion entry point:** `server/router/api/v1/user_service.go:393-455`\n   - The `DeleteUser` RPC handler calls `s.Store.DeleteUser()` at line 414\n\n2. **Store deletion logic (all three database implementations):**\n   - **MySQL:** `store/db/mysql/user_delete.go:92-127` in the `deleteUserTargetsTx` function\n   - **PostgreSQL:** `store/db/postgres/user_delete.go` (same structure)\n   - **SQLite:** `store/db/sqlite/user_delete.go` (same structure)\n\n3. **Specific reaction deletion calls in user deletion flow:**\n   - Line 96: `deleteReactionsByContentIDsTx(ctx, tx, contentIDs)` — deletes reactions **on memos created by the deleted user** (identified by memo UIDs: `\"memos/\" + memoUID`)\n   - Line 102: `deleteReactionsByCreatorTx(ctx, tx, userID)` — deletes reactions **created by the deleted user** on any content\n\n4. **Reaction deletion implementation:**\n   - `store/db/mysql/user_delete.go:417-425` — `DELETE FROM reaction WHERE content_id IN (...)` \n   - `store/db/mysql/user_delete.go:437-440` — `DELETE FROM reaction WHERE creator_id = ?`\n\n5. **Test verification:** `store/test/user_delete_test.go:97-114, 216-220`\n   - Lines 97-114 create reactions as both creator and target recipient\n   - Lines 216-220 verify that reactions on deleted user's memos are removed\n\n**No database-level constraints:** The `reaction` table schema (lines 2-9 of migration files) has no foreign key constraints on `creator_id`, so there are no cascading deletes at the database level. All cleanup is performed explicitly in code.","iteration":17,"turn":1,"type":"assistant"}
